TI  - Management of Complications After Lung Resection: Prolonged Air Leak and 
      Bronchopleural Fistula.
PG  - 347-358
LID - S1547-4127(20)30034-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.thorsurg.2020.04.008 [doi]
AB  - Prolonged air leak or alveolar-pleural fistula is common after lung resection and 
      can usually be managed with continued pleural drainage until resolution. Further 
      management options include blood patch administration, chemical pleurodesis, and 
      1-way endobronchial valve placement. Bronchopleural fistula is rare but is 
      associated with high mortality, often caused by development of concomitant 
      empyema. Bronchopleural fistula should be confirmed with bronchoscopy, which may 
      allow bronchoscopic intervention; however, transthoracic stump revision or window 
      thoracostomy may be required.
