TI  - Successful treatment of a persistent air leak with an endobronchial valve in a 
      17-year-old patient with necrotizing pneumonia.
PG  - e70053
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.70053 [doi]
LID - e70053
AB  - Pleural empyema is a severe condition associated with high morbidity and 
      mortality. Treatment usually consists of pleural drainage with chest tube or 
      surgery, in combination with antimicrobial treatment. Severe pneumonia can evolve 
      in a necrotizing pneumonia, given a higher susceptibility to the occurrence of 
      bronchopleural fistulas with persistent air leaks. This complicates recovery, and 
      surgery may not always be the optimal treatment. We present a case involving a 
      17-year-old female patient who experienced a post-operative persistent air leak 
      due to necrotizing pneumonia after video-assisted thoracic surgery decortication 
      for empyema, which was successfully treated using an endobronchial valve. After 
      6 months the valve was removed without complications. Follow-up imaging and lung 
      function revealed a limited area of atelectasis and minimal pleural thickening 
      with normal lung volumes.
